WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.195
Lesson 1.1.

00:00:02.195 --> 00:00:05.170
What DeFi is, and the risk-first mindset.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:07.211
By the end, you'll be able to name

00:00:07.211 --> 00:00:09.485
every layer of the DeFi stack, and ask

00:00:09.485 --> 00:00:12.284
the one question that matters before any yield:

00:00:12.284 --> 00:00:13.975
what am I being paid to risk?

00:00:15.275 --> 00:00:16.565
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.565 --> 00:00:19.608
First, what DeFi actually replaces, and the six

00:00:19.608 --> 00:00:22.698
layers underneath every app you'll ever use.

00:00:22.698 --> 00:00:25.076
Second, the three properties that make

00:00:25.076 --> 00:00:27.388
DeFi both powerful and dangerous.

00:00:27.388 --> 00:00:29.173
Third, the risk-first rule.

00:00:29.173 --> 00:00:32.237
And finally, a full worked example: a

00:00:32.237 --> 00:00:35.382
pool advertising 12% APY, and the five

00:00:35.382 --> 00:00:38.608
questions you ask before you believe it.

00:00:39.908 --> 00:00:40.993
The DeFi stack.

00:00:42.293 --> 00:00:44.543
Start with what DeFi replaces.

00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:48.586
Banks, brokers and exchanges are intermediaries: companies that

00:00:48.586 --> 00:00:51.728
sit in the middle of a financial transaction.

00:00:51.728 --> 00:00:55.799
DeFi replaces them with smart contracts: public programs on

00:00:55.799 --> 00:00:59.462
a blockchain that hold assets and follow fixed rules,

00:00:59.462 --> 00:01:02.108
for everyone, the same way, every time.

00:01:03.408 --> 00:01:05.583
Here's the stack, bottom to top.

00:01:05.583 --> 00:01:08.708
The blockchain records every balance and transaction.

00:01:08.708 --> 00:01:11.281
Smart contracts are the protocol logic:

00:01:11.281 --> 00:01:13.833
a lending market, an exchange pool.

00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:16.688
Tokens are the assets those contracts move.

00:01:16.688 --> 00:01:18.262
Your wallet is where you hold

00:01:18.262 --> 00:01:19.927
the keys and sign actions.

00:01:19.927 --> 00:01:23.119
The interface is the website that builds transactions

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:26.257
for you, a convenience, not the protocol itself.

00:01:26.257 --> 00:01:29.127
And data layers, explorers and dashboards, are

00:01:29.127 --> 00:01:31.447
how you verify what actually happened.

00:01:32.747 --> 00:01:34.553
A quick preview, because it matters

00:01:34.553 --> 00:01:36.307
for the whole stack above it.

00:01:36.307 --> 00:01:40.125
The blockchain layer isn't abstract: every action you take,

00:01:40.125 --> 00:01:43.816
in every layer above it, ultimately becomes a transaction

00:01:43.816 --> 00:01:46.662
that goes through this exact life cycle.

00:01:46.662 --> 00:01:49.477
You'll get the full picture in Lesson 1.2.

00:01:49.477 --> 00:01:51.243
For now, just notice that “the

00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:53.822
blockchain records it” isn't a metaphor.

00:01:53.822 --> 00:01:55.767
It's the layer everything else sits on.

00:01:57.067 --> 00:01:59.062
Notice where the interface sits.

00:01:59.062 --> 00:02:01.507
It's a convenience, not the protocol.

00:02:01.507 --> 00:02:03.675
A website can go down, get hacked,

00:02:03.675 --> 00:02:06.215
or be faked entirely, and the underlying

00:02:06.215 --> 00:02:09.117
contracts keep running exactly as written.

00:02:09.117 --> 00:02:11.393
That's also why a fake website is such

00:02:11.393 --> 00:02:14.195
an effective scam: it looks identical, but it's

00:02:14.195 --> 00:02:16.822
not the layer that actually holds your money.

00:02:18.122 --> 00:02:19.212
Quick check.

00:02:19.212 --> 00:02:20.885
Is a DeFi website the same

00:02:20.885 --> 00:02:23.116
thing as the protocol it represents?

00:02:27.536 --> 00:02:29.441
The answer: no. The interface

00:02:29.441 --> 00:02:31.836
only builds transactions for you.

00:02:31.836 --> 00:02:34.347
The smart contracts are the protocol, and

00:02:34.347 --> 00:02:37.157
interfaces can be faked or compromised without

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:38.951
touching the contracts at all.

00:02:40.251 --> 00:02:41.776
Powerful and dangerous.

00:02:43.076 --> 00:02:45.291
Property one: self-custody.

00:02:45.291 --> 00:02:47.195
In a bank, the bank can freeze your

00:02:47.195 --> 00:02:49.310
account, and the bank can also help you

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.461
recover it if something goes wrong.

00:02:51.461 --> 00:02:53.002
In your own DeFi wallet,

00:02:53.002 --> 00:02:55.089
nobody can freeze your funds.

00:02:55.089 --> 00:02:57.524
Nobody can recover them for you either.

00:02:57.524 --> 00:02:59.972
It's the same trade-off from Module Zero,

00:02:59.972 --> 00:03:02.479
and it's worth repeating here because this

00:03:02.479 --> 00:03:04.344
whole module is built around it.

00:03:05.644 --> 00:03:08.349
One sobering number on self-custody, from

00:03:08.349 --> 00:03:10.904
outside research, not this program.

00:03:10.904 --> 00:03:15.426
Blockchain-analytics researchers have estimated that somewhere around a

00:03:15.426 --> 00:03:17.938
fifth of all bitcoin ever mined sits in

00:03:17.938 --> 00:03:22.084
wallets that are effectively lost: forgotten passwords, discarded

00:03:22.084 --> 00:03:25.524
hard drives, seed phrases nobody can find anymore.

00:03:25.524 --> 00:03:27.289
Nobody can recover it.

00:03:27.289 --> 00:03:29.214
Nobody can freeze it either.

00:03:29.214 --> 00:03:31.769
Same coin, both directions, permanently.

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:35.299
Property two: composability.

00:03:35.299 --> 00:03:39.199
DeFi protocols plug into each other, like building blocks.

00:03:39.199 --> 00:03:42.839
A stablecoin can be deposited into a lending market.

00:03:42.839 --> 00:03:45.857
The interest-bearing receipt from that lending market

00:03:45.857 --> 00:03:48.169
can be deposited into a yield vault.

00:03:48.169 --> 00:03:49.576
That vault's shares can be

00:03:49.576 --> 00:03:51.648
used as collateral somewhere else.

00:03:51.648 --> 00:03:54.979
Composability is what makes DeFi powerful, letting

00:03:54.979 --> 00:03:58.087
small pieces combine into complex products.

00:03:58.087 --> 00:04:00.898
It's also why one position can fail because of

00:04:00.898 --> 00:04:04.307
something several layers away that it quietly depends on.

00:04:05.607 --> 00:04:07.777
Property three: transparency.

00:04:07.777 --> 00:04:11.382
Every DeFi transaction and every contract's code can be

00:04:11.382 --> 00:04:14.837
checked by anyone, for free, on a block explorer.

00:04:14.837 --> 00:04:16.582
That's genuinely powerful.

00:04:16.582 --> 00:04:18.687
But transparent doesn't mean safe.

00:04:18.687 --> 00:04:20.969
A contract can be fully visible and

00:04:20.969 --> 00:04:23.125
still contain a bug, an admin key

00:04:23.125 --> 00:04:25.661
that can change the rules, or economics

00:04:25.661 --> 00:04:28.307
that only work while a subsidy lasts.

00:04:28.307 --> 00:04:31.257
Transparency gives you the ability to check.

00:04:31.257 --> 00:04:32.452
It doesn't check for you.

00:04:33.752 --> 00:04:35.364
Here's a real example of

00:04:35.364 --> 00:04:38.307
composability biting, not a hypothetical.

00:04:38.307 --> 00:04:42.034
In May of two thousand twenty-two, during the collapse

00:04:42.034 --> 00:04:45.693
of the Terra ecosystem, a liquid staking token called

00:04:45.693 --> 00:04:48.945
stETH briefly traded below the value of the ETH

00:04:48.945 --> 00:04:52.401
it represented, on the open market, even though it

00:04:52.401 --> 00:04:55.547
was still redeemable one-to-one over time.

00:04:55.547 --> 00:04:59.319
Protocols that had accepted stETH as collateral, several

00:04:59.319 --> 00:05:02.496
layers away from Terra itself, felt real stress

00:05:02.496 --> 00:05:05.144
from an event that, on the surface, had

00:05:05.144 --> 00:05:06.732
nothing to do with them.

00:05:08.032 --> 00:05:09.122
Quick check.

00:05:09.122 --> 00:05:11.964
Why can composability increase risk, even in a

00:05:11.964 --> 00:05:14.504
position that looks simple on the surface?

00:05:18.924 --> 00:05:21.761
The answer: a position inherits the failure

00:05:21.761 --> 00:05:24.470
risk of every protocol, asset, oracle and

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:26.791
bridge it depends on, even the ones

00:05:26.791 --> 00:05:28.919
you can't see from the front end.

00:05:30.219 --> 00:05:31.404
The risk-first rule.

00:05:32.704 --> 00:05:34.739
Apply that rule to the stETH

00:05:34.739 --> 00:05:36.864
example from a moment ago.

00:05:36.864 --> 00:05:40.860
A risk-first operator, before ever depositing stETH as

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:44.856
collateral anywhere, would have asked: what happens to

00:05:44.856 --> 00:05:47.979
this position if stETH trades below ETH on

00:05:47.979 --> 00:05:50.579
the open market, even briefly?

00:05:50.579 --> 00:05:53.418
That single question, asked in advance, is the

00:05:53.418 --> 00:05:56.681
entire difference between being surprised by an event

00:05:56.681 --> 00:05:58.614
and having already sized for it.

00:05:59.914 --> 00:06:02.774
Here's the rule this whole program is built on.

00:06:02.774 --> 00:06:04.742
Before comparing returns, list what

00:06:04.742 --> 00:06:06.464
could make you lose money.

00:06:06.464 --> 00:06:09.124
A higher yield means you're being paid to carry

00:06:09.124 --> 00:06:12.194
more risk, whether or not the website mentions it.

00:06:12.194 --> 00:06:14.709
If you can't name the risk, you don't understand

00:06:14.709 --> 00:06:17.122
the yield, no matter how good the number looks.

00:06:18.422 --> 00:06:20.342
Here's the difference between the

00:06:20.342 --> 00:06:22.167
two mindsets, side by side.

00:06:22.167 --> 00:06:24.969
Return-first starts with the number, asks how to

00:06:24.969 --> 00:06:26.971
get more of it, and treats risk as

00:06:26.971 --> 00:06:29.272
a footnote to check later, if ever.

00:06:29.272 --> 00:06:32.356
Risk-first starts with what could go wrong, sizes the

00:06:32.356 --> 00:06:35.326
position around that answer, and only then looks at

00:06:35.326 --> 00:06:37.967
whether the remaining return is worth it.

00:06:37.967 --> 00:06:39.362
Same opportunity.

00:06:39.362 --> 00:06:41.709
Completely different order of operations.

00:06:43.009 --> 00:06:45.342
Here's what asking the question actually

00:06:45.342 --> 00:06:47.064
looks like, step by step.

00:06:47.064 --> 00:06:48.329
You see a yield.

00:06:48.329 --> 00:06:49.562
You ask what could make

00:06:49.562 --> 00:06:51.404
it fail, before anything else.

00:06:51.404 --> 00:06:53.200
You name every risk you can

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.809
find: contract, oracle, bridge, peg.

00:06:55.809 --> 00:06:57.446
You size the position so that if

00:06:57.446 --> 00:06:59.581
you're wrong, it doesn't matter much.

00:06:59.581 --> 00:07:00.906
And only then do you

00:07:00.906 --> 00:07:02.546
deposit, or decide not to.

00:07:03.846 --> 00:07:04.851
Worked example.

00:07:06.151 --> 00:07:08.965
Question one, and the most important: where

00:07:08.965 --> 00:07:11.431
does the 12% actually come from?

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:14.739
Every DeFi return is a mix of five sources.

00:07:14.739 --> 00:07:17.064
Service fees, paid by traders.

00:07:17.064 --> 00:07:19.184
Interest, paid by borrowers.

00:07:19.184 --> 00:07:21.434
Security rewards, for staking.

00:07:21.434 --> 00:07:24.310
Structural carry, like funding and basis.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:27.151
And incentives: emissions or points, which are

00:07:27.151 --> 00:07:29.385
only real once you've sold them.

00:07:29.385 --> 00:07:31.997
If the answer is “incentives,” that

00:07:31.997 --> 00:07:33.665
12% has an expiry date.

00:07:34.965 --> 00:07:37.570
So let's actually decompose an example

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:39.940
12%, the way an operator would.

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:42.970
Say 2% comes from real trading fees.

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:45.590
1% from real borrower interest.

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:48.702
And the remaining 9%, the biggest slice by far,

00:07:48.702 --> 00:07:52.503
from a reward token being emitted to attract deposits.

00:07:52.503 --> 00:07:55.314
That 9% only exists while the emissions keep

00:07:55.314 --> 00:07:58.126
flowing, and it's only real once you've sold

00:07:58.126 --> 00:08:00.675
the reward token for something else.

00:08:00.675 --> 00:08:02.609
The headline number and the durable

00:08:02.609 --> 00:08:04.597
number are two very different things.

00:08:05.897 --> 00:08:08.662
And four more questions, before you deposit.

00:08:08.662 --> 00:08:11.607
Which stablecoin, and what actually backs it?

00:08:11.607 --> 00:08:15.202
You'll learn to judge that properly in Lesson 1.5.

00:08:15.202 --> 00:08:16.883
Which contracts hold the money,

00:08:16.883 --> 00:08:18.617
and who can upgrade them?

00:08:18.617 --> 00:08:21.003
Is there a bridge or an oracle involved,

00:08:21.003 --> 00:08:23.577
each one another thing that can fail?

00:08:23.577 --> 00:08:25.080
And how do you withdraw, and

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:27.262
could withdrawals ever be blocked?

00:08:27.262 --> 00:08:30.234
If you can't answer these five questions, you don't

00:08:30.234 --> 00:08:32.292
know what the 12% is paying you for.

00:08:33.592 --> 00:08:34.632
One more.

00:08:34.632 --> 00:08:36.976
If 9 of a pool's 12% APY comes

00:08:36.976 --> 00:08:40.529
from a reward token being emitted, what should

00:08:40.529 --> 00:08:43.327
you assume about that 9%? [[pause 4]]

00:08:47.747 --> 00:08:49.812
The answer: it's not durable.

00:08:49.812 --> 00:08:53.166
It only exists while the emissions continue, and it's

00:08:53.166 --> 00:08:56.457
only real once you've actually sold the reward token.

00:08:57.757 --> 00:08:58.942
Last check.

00:08:58.942 --> 00:09:01.702
In one sentence, what is the risk-first mindset?

00:09:06.122 --> 00:09:08.567
The answer: identify what could make you

00:09:08.567 --> 00:09:10.892
lose money, before you compare returns.

00:09:12.192 --> 00:09:13.652
Here's your checklist.

00:09:13.652 --> 00:09:15.187
Do it now, for real.

00:09:15.187 --> 00:09:16.796
First, you can name the six

00:09:16.796 --> 00:09:18.532
layers of the DeFi stack.

00:09:18.532 --> 00:09:22.237
Second, you can explain why self-custody cuts both ways.

00:09:22.237 --> 00:09:24.341
And third, before any yield, you ask:

00:09:24.341 --> 00:09:25.947
what am I being paid to risk?

00:09:27.247 --> 00:09:28.492
Let's recap.

00:09:28.492 --> 00:09:33.343
DeFi replaces intermediaries with smart contracts, across six layers:

00:09:33.343 --> 00:09:38.147
blockchain, contracts, tokens, wallet, interface and data layers.

00:09:38.147 --> 00:09:41.489
Self-custody, composability and transparency make

00:09:41.489 --> 00:09:43.861
it both powerful and dangerous.

00:09:43.861 --> 00:09:46.450
And the risk-first rule: list what could make

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:49.151
you lose money, before you ever compare returns.

00:09:50.451 --> 00:09:52.966
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:09:52.966 --> 00:09:55.226
You now have the whole lesson in your

00:09:55.226 --> 00:09:58.379
head: the six-layer stack, the three properties that

00:09:58.379 --> 00:10:01.650
make DeFi powerful and dangerous, the risk-first rule,

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:04.746
and how to actually decompose a headline yield.

00:10:04.746 --> 00:10:08.778
Next up, Lesson 1.2: How a transaction actually happens.
